  "worked_solution": "We have a vertical tangent when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${x}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta $ where ${x}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$\n\nBy writing down and solving the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ) & =0 \\\\ \\theta  & =\\pi n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is any integer. However, among the solutions above, only one belongs to the required domain, namely\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\pi .\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether our candidate has ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solution into ${y}^{′}(\\theta )$ and check that ${y}^{′}(\\theta )\\ne 0.$ If ${y}^{′}(\\theta )=0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting ${\\theta }_{1}=\\pi ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =5(-1) \\\\  & =-5 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${\\theta }_{1}=\\pi $ is a valid solution.\n\nSo, our curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\pi .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14267441/assets/adac21da6cdbbf96f049649124f87ddc19153d22b1fcdb51e39893b972a5a2bf.png)",
